Assistant: View protection rules affect development potential by constraining building heights, setbacks, and massing to preserve sight lines and visual corridors. These regulations reduce the developable envelope on affected parcels, lowering potential floor area ratios and consequently land values. The economic impact depends on the stringency of restrictions, local demand elasticities for vertical development, and the availability of transferable development rights or other compensatory mechanisms. Empirical studies show property value impacts ranging from 15-40% reductions for heavily constrained parcels, though neighboring properties may experience offsetting appreciation from preserved amenities. The net welfare effects involve weighing the capitalized value of protected views against foregone agglomeration benefits and housing supply constraints.

